  "worked_solution": "First, we apply the sum rule and write our repeated integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{2}^{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}3{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z-{\\int }_{2}^{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}4x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\n$$\n\nSince the domain of integration $R=[0,1]\\times [1,2]\\times [2,5]$ is a rectangular box, and each integrand can be expressed as $f(x)\\cdot g(y)\\cdot h(z),$ we can rewrite our sum of repeated integrals as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\text{d}x{\\int }_{1}^{2}3{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\text{d}z-{\\int }_{0}^{1}4x\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\text{d}y{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\text{d}z\n$$\n\nLet's evaluate each of these separately:\n\n- For the first product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\text{d}x{\\int }_{1}^{2}3{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\text{d}z & =(1-0)\\cdot [{y}^{3}]_{1}^{2}\\cdot (5-2) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (8-1)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =3\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =21.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}4x\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\text{d}y{\\int }_{2}^{5}\\text{d}z & =[2{x}^{2}]_{0}^{1}\\cdot (2-1)\\cdot (5-2) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}(3{y}^{2}-1)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z=21-6=15.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
